        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>Cytokines, proteins that serve as messengers to activate immune responses in lymphocytes and macrophages, appear to be altered during spaceflight. Because of this suppression of the immune system in otherwise healthy subjects, spaceflight offers a unique opportunity to study the effects of biological substances without the complications of illnesses normally present in immunosuppressed subjects. Polyethylene Glycol-Interleukin 2 (PEG-IL-2) is an immunological mediator and enhancer developed and manufactured by Chiron Corporation. The purpose of this experiment in conjunction with the Immune 2 experiment was to confirm and define the ability of Polyethylene Glycol-Interleukin 2 (PEG-IL2) to prevent or ameliorate the detrimental effects of space flight on immune responses of rats.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>Half of the animals in each group were injected intravenously with PEG-IL-2 two to three hours before transfer to the Animal Enclosure Modules (AEMs). Upon recovery of flight subjects, the following tissues and organs were removed and weighed: brain, heart, kidneys, liver, lungs, trachea, spleen, and thymus. Blood samples were collected and analyzed for blood cell counts and differentials. They were also analyzed for corticosterone concentration with radioimmunoassay. Macrophage (M-CSF) and granulocyte-macrophage stimulating factor (GM-CSF) dependent macrophage colony formation from bone marrow cells was assayed. Lymphocytes were obtained from the spleen and lymph nodes and assayed to determine cell proliferation rates and the secretion of cytokines, M-CSF, IL-6, IFN-g, and TGF-§. Peritoneal macrophages were assayed for secretion of TNFa and IL-6. The femur and tibia were collected and underwent biomechanical testing to calculate strength (N), deflection (mm), and energy  (mJ). Bones were then dried and ashed in order to assess mineral content.<P><B>Results:</B><P>The results of this experiment are joined with the Immune 2 experiment. There was no significant pattern observed for body weight and organ weight parameters. Additionally, few immunological parameters were consistent across the  Immune 1 and Immune 2 experiments, making conclusive observations difficult. Significant changes in bone parameters were observed in Immune 2, but not Immune 1. The inconsistencies between the two experiments prevented any conclusive evidence concerning the effectiveness of the PEG-IL-2 treatment. Several factors may have played a role in these discrepancies. Some of the animals flown were found to have damage to their tails upon recovery (necrosis, loss, and gangrenous tissues).  However, compared to the animals in the same group, these changes did not correlate with any of the parameters measured.  While there were the expected trends in the Immune 1 experiments, the control animals did not exhibit as many of the flight-related changes as were anticipated.  In contrast, the control group in the Immune 2 study did have more dramatic changes associated with spaceflight. These differences in the control groups may have been due to the fact that flight animals in the Immune 2 experiment were exposed to significantly higher ambient temperatures than animals in Immune 1.
        </div>

